BrailleBridge — Mechanical Design Notes

1. Overview

BrailleBridge is a proposed modular, repairable and low-cost refreshable Braille display. Its mechanical system is designed to convert electronic control signals into tactile Braille patterns using movable pins.

The initial prototype focuses on a single six-dot Braille cell. Each dot must be independently controllable so that different combinations of raised and lowered pins can represent supported Braille characters.

The mechanical design will prioritise reliable pin movement, correct dot alignment, ease of assembly, maintainability and future expansion.

Development status: Mechanical concept and design planning. Final dimensions, linkage geometry, materials and operating performance require CAD development and physical validation.

2. Six-Dot Braille Cell Layout

A standard six-dot Braille cell consists of two columns and three rows.

The dot numbering is arranged as follows:

Left column    Right column
    1              4
    2              5
    3              6

Each dot has two intended states:

* Raised: The dot is presented as part of the tactile character.
* Lowered: The dot remains below the raised-dot level.

Different combinations of these six dots represent different Braille characters.

The six-dot layout will guide the design of the pin guide, actuator arrangement and control mapping.

3. Proposed Mechanical Architecture

The proposed mechanical assembly consists of the following elements:

3.1 Braille Pins

Six tactile pins form the output surface of the initial prototype.

The pins must:

* Move vertically with minimal friction.
* Remain aligned with the standard six-dot layout.
* Provide consistent raised and lowered positions.
* Resist unwanted movement during tactile reading.
* Be removable for inspection or replacement.

Pin dimensions, tip geometry, spacing and travel will be determined during CAD development and user-oriented testing.

3.2 Pin Guide

A custom pin guide is proposed to maintain the alignment of all six pins.

The guide is intended to:

* Constrain unwanted sideways movement.
* Maintain consistent spacing between pins.
* Reduce friction and binding.
* Support repeatable pin travel.
* Allow access for maintenance.

A 3D-printed PETG component is a candidate for the pin guide. Its suitability will be evaluated through dimensional checks, wear testing and repeated operation.

3.3 Servo Actuation

The proposed electronic architecture uses six MG90S micro servos for the initial cell.

Each servo is intended to actuate one Braille dot through a mechanical linkage or pushrod.

The mechanical design must translate servo rotation into controlled pin movement.

The final linkage geometry must be developed to ensure:

* Sufficient pin travel.
* Reliable movement between the raised and lowered states.
* Minimal unwanted movement of neighbouring pins.
* No excessive force on the pins or servo gears.
* Adequate clearance throughout the operating range.

Independent control of all six dots must be demonstrated before the mechanism can be considered functional.

3.4 Servo Mounting Structure

A mounting frame will position the servos relative to the pin guide.

The frame must provide:

* Stable servo mounting.
* Repeatable actuator alignment.
* Clearance for linkages and moving components.
* Access for servicing or replacing individual servos.
* A compact arrangement suitable for a modular cell.

The mounting design will be refined after the linkage and pin geometry are established.

4. Mechanical Motion Concept

The intended motion sequence is:

1. The firmware identifies the required six-dot Braille pattern.
2. The controller sends position commands to the servo driver.
3. The selected servos move their respective linkages.
4. The linkages raise the required pins and lower the remaining pins.
5. The resulting pin arrangement represents the requested character.
6. The system updates the pins when the user navigates to another character.

The final design must ensure that the mechanism can transition between different patterns without pin collisions, jamming or unintended movement.

This motion sequence describes the intended operation. Actual movement and repeatability must be verified during prototype testing.

